  "review_flags_scope": "Only the named F/V side conventions, actual Gauss successor transport, and K empty-cusp parameter/case independence reviewed here. Not a complete def:walls acceptance.",
  "reason": "The three new modules are faithful for their stated partial scope. I inspected their exact definitions, theorem domains and proof bodies against the named-wall source. No arbitrary sign, adjacency or empty-cusp oracle is introduced. Generic-family transport concerns the actual constructed complete visit cycle and its actual crossing supports.",
  "findings": [
    "F: FlatRightSide means turn j is −1 at every point of that actual half interval, and FlatLeftSide means +1. The iff-at theorems use genuine generic-side chi constancy, so a single selected point is equivalent to the full side condition. flat_named_sides uses the actual turn SignChanges witness and its proved independent-side sign comparison to obtain a unique right-side Bool whose complement is the left side. It does not identify the right side with positive or negative parameter time in advance, and it imposes no derivative condition.",
    "V: contactSign is exactly chi(a,a+1,M) at the actual negative-parameter side, using sideBase only as a representative. contactSign_eq_at proves equality at any negative-side parameter. vertex_contact_signs derives nonzero contact sign and the opposite positive-side chi sign for independently chosen side points from the source SignChanges premise. The proved contact-mark uniqueness identifies M and a for two vertex-edge germs with the same labelled centre. The central neighbour-sign equality/inequality then preserves the actual BigonAt/SlidingAt subtype. This does not assert that the oriented contact sign is determined by the centre alone.",
    "Actual cyclic transport: list_next_map_injective uses the proved modulo-length successor formula for every list position, including last-to-first wraparound. Nodup and injectivity ensure that mapping neither merges nor reorders visits. The Cycle result descends through actual list rotations. The abstract equivalence helper is instantiated with the already proved canonical full gaussCycle transport for generic families and cyclic parent relabelling; no desired cycle equality is an added source hypothesis. Both directions of GaussVisitsAdjacent are preserved. Membership excludes an empty cycle, and the successor proof does not drop the two-visit case.",
    "K: CuspEmptyAt requires an actual cusp witness, the actual central CuspCase and adjacency of its two actual newborn visits on the proven loop side at sideBase. The generic-family visit transport retains crossing support and edge identity, so it carries these endpoints to precisely the new endpoint visits. cusp_empty_parameter_iff therefore compares arbitrary loop-side parameters. cuspEmptyAt_iff_at uses the proved unique central case to eliminate any alternate Boolean witness, then proves equivalence to adjacency at any chosen loop-side point. Proof irrelevance changes only proofs, not the chosen case, support, parameter or side. The underlying Gauss word is the position-preserving crossing-label map of the actual visit cycle, and the newborn has exactly these two visits, so this remains the source cyclic-word adjacency test. The inherited empty-middle-edge consequence holds on both sides without assuming that all cusps are empty."
  ],
  "not_certified": [
    "The full original def:walls row remains pending; no accepted original claim is added by this report.",
    "Complete cyclic compatibility of all named-wall predicates/conventions and CuspEmptyAt itself is not yet certified here. The actual Gauss-adjacency shift lemma is supporting machinery, not a substitute for that complete transport.",
    "Unordered T/C witness permutation invariance, any remaining named/silent conventions, and the full definition aggregate with its final semantic/type/axiom receipt remain separate review obligations.",
    "No classification of arbitrary singular germs, no new main state-sum wall law, and no soft-theorem result is asserted."
  ],
